    <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Developers and system administrators frequently look to clean text directly inside local source code editors. While effective, desktop editors require specific steps to ensure duplicates are fully purged rather than simply reorganized.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-200 mt-4 mb-2">1. Notepad++ Setup</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Performing a <strong>notepad++ remove duplicate lines</strong> workflow requires attention: selecting <code>Edit &rarr; Line Operations &rarr; Sort Lines Lexicographically</code> merely places identical lines adjacent to one another—it does not delete them automatically. To completely strip redundant lines, install the popular <em>TextFX</em> plugin (<code>TextFX &rarr; TextFX Tools &rarr; Sort lines, only UNIQUE</code>) or execute a targeted regular expression search. Applying a <strong>regex remove duplicate lines</strong> pattern inside the Find and Replace menu (with regular expression mode enabled) helps match repeated line blocks:
    </p>
    <pre class="bg-slate-900 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-emerald-400 font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto"><code>Find: ^(.*?)$\s+?^(?=[\s\S]*?^\1$)
Replace: (leave empty)</code></pre>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-200 mt-4 mb-2">2. Visual Studio Code Workflows</h3>
