    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-100 mb-4">Command-Line Solutions: Linux, Bash, and Stream Editors</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">
      On Unix-like systems, terminal utilities provide direct manipulation for disk-based files without launching a graphical user interface.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed mb-3">
      When addressing <strong>linux remove duplicate lines</strong> or configuring <strong>bash remove duplicate lines</strong> automated scripts, the common approach pairs sort with uniqueness filters:
    </p>
    <pre class="bg-slate-900 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-emerald-400 font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto"><code>sort raw_list.txt | uniq &gt; cleaned_list.txt</code></pre>
    <p class="leading-relaxed mb-3 text-sm">
      Note that <code>uniq</code> requires adjacent matching lines, meaning sorting is mandatory. To resolve <strong>linux remove duplicate lines from file</strong> requirements while preserving original line order, Awk offers an efficient alternative: <code>awk '!seen[$0]++' raw_list.txt &gt; output.txt</code>.
